# XSH ftell(), ftello()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.ftell.001
anchor     functions/ftell.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       On a stream reading "0123456789", ftell() is 0, then 3 after three
           reads, 7 after fseek(f, 7, SEEK_SET); on a "w" stream it is 5 after
           fputs("hello") (still buffered); on an "r+" stream it is 2 after
           a read and fputc() of one byte (after fseek()); on an "a" stream on
           a 4-byte file it is 6 after fputs("xy") and fflush().
test       t/001-position.c

assertion  XSH.ftell.002
anchor     functions/ftell.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       With errno set to ERANGE, a successful ftell() on a file stream
           leaves errno ERANGE.
note       The change history does not say which edition added this, so
           "since" is left out.
test       t/002-errno-kept.c

assertion  XSH.ftell.003
anchor     functions/ftell.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       ftello() is 3 after three reads of a file stream; after fseeko(f,
           (off_t)1 << 33, SEEK_SET) it is 2^33 (when off_t has more than 33
           bits).
test       t/003-ftello.c

assertion  XSH.ftell.004
anchor     functions/ftell.html#ERRORS/ESPIPE
class      required
says       ftell() and ftello() on streams from fdopen() of a pipe's read end and
           of a FIFO opened O_RDONLY|O_NONBLOCK return -1 with errno
           ESPIPE.
note       "since" is left out: the change history shows only that the
           error predates Issue 6.
test       t/004-espipe-pipe.c

assertion  XSH.ftell.005
anchor     functions/ftell.html#ERRORS/ESPIPE
class      required
since      2001
says       ftell() and ftello() on a stream from fdopen() of one end of an AF_UNIX
           socketpair() return -1 with errno ESPIPE.
note       Issue 6 added sockets to this error.
test       t/005-espipe-socket.c

assertion  XSH.ftell.006
anchor     functions/ftell.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      required
says       On a stream reading a file whose descriptor has been closed with
           close() after one read and an ftell(), ftell() and ftello()
           return -1 with errno EBADF.
note       close() does not move the offset, so the descriptor is not a
           second handle (XSH 2.5.1). "since" is left out: the change
           history shows only that the error predates Issue 6.
test       t/006-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.ftell.007
anchor     functions/ftell.html#ERRORS/EOVERFLOW
class      required
since      2001
says       Where off_t is wider than long: after fseeko() to (off_t)LONG_MAX + 10
           on a file stream, ftell() returns -1 with errno EOVERFLOW. Otherwise
           NOTINUSE.
test       t/007-eoverflow.c
